You can also author the same workflow as YAML and load it back:

<CodeFromFile file="examples/rust/workflows/research.yaml" lang="yaml" title="research.yaml" />

```python
from wyrd import Workflow
wf = Workflow.from_path("research.yaml")
```

`Workflow.from_path` loads local Agent and Prompt files next to the Workflow
without a server. Steps that reference registered Agents, as this file does,
are read from the Wyrd server using your configured credentials.

## Run

```python
run = wf.run({"topic": "the Rust borrow checker"})
print(run.status)    # "succeeded", "failed", "cancelled", or "timed_out"
print(run.outputs)   # named outputs declared with with_outputs
print(run.steps["planner"]["structured_output"])
```

Mapping input supplies declared inputs by name; undeclared keys and values of the wrong declared type are rejected. A string is shorthand for the declared string input named `input`. Validation, input, and route errors raise `WyrdError` before any step runs. Once the run starts, step failures are recorded in `run.steps` and `run.error` instead of being raised.

## Save And Load

```python
wf.save("research.yaml")
loaded = Workflow.from_path("research.yaml")
assert Workflow.from_yaml(wf.to_yaml()).to_yaml() == wf.to_yaml()
```

## Custom DAG

```python
wf = Workflow(name="pipeline")
wf.add(research_agent)
wf.add_after(analysis_agent, after=research_agent)
wf.add_after(summary_agent, after=research_agent)
wf.add_after(final_agent, after=[analysis_agent, summary_agent])
wf.with_step_inputs("final", {
    "analysis": "steps.analysis.output.text",
    "summary": "steps.summary.output.text",
})
wf.with_outputs({"report": "steps.final.output.text"})
```

## Parallel

Use `parallel` when steps do not depend on each other. Use `add_after` to fan back in.

